// repros/dyn_growth_with_window.flx — growing a dyn while a window exists
//
//   ./fluxa run repros/dyn_growth_with_window.flx
//
// Growing a `dyn` past its end is documented and works. Doing it after a
// graphics window has been created segfaults — in this shape, every time.
//
// ── what it is not ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Not raylib, as far as the stack can tell. gdb, on the crash:
//
//   Thread 1 "fluxa" received signal SIGSEGV
//   #0  value_release_data ()
//   #1  value_release_data ()
//   #2  eval ()
//   #3  eval ()
//   #4  eval ()
//   #5  runtime_exec_persist.part ()
//   #6  run_once ()
//   #7  main ()
//
// No raylib frame anywhere, and the fault is in the runtime's own value
// release path, reached from evaluating the assignment. What the window seems
// to do is expose it rather than cause it.
//
// Nor is it allocation pressure or GL memory: two 1024x1024 images, made with
// image.new and never discarded until the end, do not trigger it at all.
//
// ── what it is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Six runs each, one thing changed at a time:
//
//   growth alone, no libs ........................... 6/6 survive
//   growth after image.new(1024,1024) twice ......... 6/6 survive
//   a window, no growth ............................. 6/6 survive
//   growth FIRST, window opened afterwards .......... 6/6 survive
//   window FIRST, then growth ....................... 0/6 survive
//
// The order is the whole thing: the same two statements in the other order are
// stable. Growing less makes it flakier rather than safer — at twenty elements
// it survived eight of eight, at forty seven, at eighty five, at a hundred and
// sixty three — which is what a stale pointer to a reallocated buffer looks
// like, hit or missed depending on what the allocator does next.
//
// Found while writing a call that takes a list of points: an artwork building
// that list in a loop, with the stage already up, is exactly this program.

import std graph

dyn win = graph.init(400, 300, "repro")

dyn p = [0.0]
int k = 1
while k < 160 {
    p[k] = 1.0
    k = k + 1
}

print("survived, len = ", len(p))
graph.close(win)
